WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.235 --> 00:00:02.981
- My whole life is a very funny story.

00:00:02.981 --> 00:00:05.089
(gentle music)

00:00:05.089 --> 00:00:07.582
You want me to introduce myself?

00:00:07.582 --> 00:00:09.832
My name is David S. Wisnia.

00:00:12.141 --> 00:00:15.391
I am 90 years old, a Holocaust survivor

00:00:16.262 --> 00:00:19.345
of close to three years in Auschwitz.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.083
When I arrived in Auschwitz, itself,

00:00:23.083 --> 00:00:26.583
right after being tattooed with my number,

00:00:27.911 --> 00:00:30.439
I looked up at the SS man before that,

00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:32.426
who was standing in front of me.

00:00:32.426 --> 00:00:36.117
My eyes were fixed into his belt buckle.

00:00:36.117 --> 00:00:39.867
"Gott mit uns," which
means "God is with us."

00:00:41.021 --> 00:00:42.633
But I said to myself, "God is not

00:00:42.633 --> 00:00:46.492
with you and I'm going to outlive you."

00:00:46.492 --> 00:00:49.353
The first couple of weeks
in Auschwitz were murder,

00:00:49.353 --> 00:00:53.904
literally murder, because my
job was collecting bodies.

00:00:53.904 --> 00:00:57.936
One night, about two
weeks of dragging bodies,

00:00:57.936 --> 00:01:01.748
the cell block leader
says, "Who can sing?"

00:01:01.748 --> 00:01:04.819
Of course, everybody knew Wisnia sang.

00:01:04.819 --> 00:01:07.254
I got down and performed,

00:01:07.254 --> 00:01:11.182
and that made me a privileged prisoner.

00:01:11.182 --> 00:01:14.932
I completely, totally
removed my past from me

00:01:15.843 --> 00:01:18.970
when I came to Hoboken, New Jersey,

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:22.288
and I became a full-fledged canter.

00:01:22.288 --> 00:01:27.188
I composed a couple of
songs that are in the museum

00:01:27.188 --> 00:01:29.335
in Washington, while in Auschwitz.

00:01:29.335 --> 00:01:32.241
One of them is in Polish, about Auschwitz.

00:01:32.241 --> 00:01:35.074
(sings in Polish)

00:01:54.012 --> 00:01:58.095
December, 1944, Nazis
began evacuating Auschwitz.

00:01:59.693 --> 00:02:01.803
I was on one of the transports.

00:02:01.803 --> 00:02:05.715
Arriving in Dachau, I realized
I could not survive Dachau,

00:02:05.715 --> 00:02:09.558
because in Auschwitz, I
was privileged prisoner.

00:02:09.558 --> 00:02:12.940
I was looking for a way
to get out of Dachau.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.607
I decided I was going to escape.

00:02:16.901 --> 00:02:20.669
As we were emptying the
railroad cars into the ditches,

00:02:20.669 --> 00:02:22.683
I found the shovel there.

00:02:22.683 --> 00:02:24.581
Anyway, I made my way.

00:02:24.581 --> 00:02:28.224
I walked towards the
fire, towards the front,

00:02:28.224 --> 00:02:31.098
not knowing exactly where I was going.

00:02:31.098 --> 00:02:34.598
I heard a roar off a huge column of tanks.

00:02:36.966 --> 00:02:39.648
The whole column stopped for me.

00:02:39.648 --> 00:02:42.951
Out of the hatch comes out James L. Walker

00:02:42.951 --> 00:02:44.618
from South Carolina.

00:02:45.529 --> 00:02:48.731
I asked him to get me
somebody who speaks Polish.

00:02:48.731 --> 00:02:51.874
He was worse than the
one who spoke English.

00:02:51.874 --> 00:02:55.124
Anyway, I became a part of that outfit,

00:02:55.964 --> 00:03:00.355
never left them until I
came to the United States.

00:03:00.355 --> 00:03:04.522
Because I speak German, I
talked down, towards the end

00:03:05.598 --> 00:03:08.431
of April and the beginning of May,

00:03:09.806 --> 00:03:14.209
in Austria, some of the SS
to throw away their arms,

00:03:14.209 --> 00:03:16.081
and I made sure that they understood

00:03:16.081 --> 00:03:19.581
that we would treat them as war prisoners.

00:03:21.338 --> 00:03:22.838
"Kriegsgefangene,"

00:03:24.048 --> 00:03:26.798
and that's exactly what happened.

00:03:28.391 --> 00:03:32.594
I came home and found my
family dead, massacred,

00:03:32.594 --> 00:03:34.477
and to the best of my knowledge,

00:03:34.477 --> 00:03:38.394
I eventually found out
that my father resisted,

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.147
and they found the gun,

00:03:44.054 --> 00:03:45.739
and they killed my whole family.

00:03:45.739 --> 00:03:50.320
I really now understand,
maybe not for the same reason,

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:53.051
when the Pope comes to the United States

00:03:53.051 --> 00:03:55.480
or any place, he kisses the ground.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:57.883
Well, let me tell you,
I kissed the ground of

00:03:57.883 --> 00:04:00.603
the United States, because I

00:04:00.603 --> 00:04:04.222
really understand what
America's all about.

00:04:04.222 --> 00:04:08.389
What I have, thank God, is
a beautiful family here.

00:04:09.477 --> 00:04:12.513
A wife, now, for 69 years, and I love

00:04:12.513 --> 00:04:14.974
to say I still have hope.

00:04:14.974 --> 00:04:16.968
That's her name, Hope,

00:04:16.968 --> 00:04:21.832
and my grandson, thank God,
happens to be quite a pianist.

00:04:21.832 --> 00:04:25.999
He finally, after many tries
of all kinds of composers,

00:04:27.845 --> 00:04:31.997
you know, to translate my
song from Polish to English,

00:04:31.997 --> 00:04:34.296
he did it about a couple of months ago,

00:04:34.296 --> 00:04:35.828
and now we have it in English

00:04:35.828 --> 00:04:38.591
and I'm going to perform it tonight.

00:04:38.591 --> 00:04:39.424
Go ahead.

00:04:44.647 --> 00:04:47.531
♫ I remember it so clearly

00:04:47.531 --> 00:04:50.698
♫ My days in Oświęcim

00:04:51.908 --> 00:04:55.396
♫ Got out alive just nearly

00:04:55.396 --> 00:04:59.534
♫ And still the name it stings

00:04:59.534 --> 00:05:01.814
♫ Still I see that place

00:05:01.814 --> 00:05:05.981
♫ Enduring like the scars I can't erase

00:05:08.464 --> 00:05:12.053
♫ Appel, Abstand, Mutzen ab

00:05:12.053 --> 00:05:13.167
That's German.

00:05:13.167 --> 00:05:17.141
♫ Hear the sergeant's call

00:05:17.141 --> 00:05:19.376
♫ You'll wish you were

00:05:19.376 --> 00:05:22.793
♫ Just never born at all

00:05:25.393 --> 00:05:27.506
♫ Oświęcim

00:05:27.506 --> 00:05:29.790
♫ Through pain and sickness

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:31.036
♫ Always working

00:05:31.036 --> 00:05:33.781
♫ Oświęcim

00:05:33.781 --> 00:05:36.562
♫ With death behind you always lurking

00:05:36.562 --> 00:05:38.065
♫ Oświęcim

00:05:38.065 --> 00:05:39.075
♫ Oświęcim

00:05:39.075 --> 00:05:41.746
♫ Evil like some twisted dream

00:05:41.746 --> 00:05:44.358
♫ Nevermind the hope of fleeing

00:05:44.358 --> 00:05:47.060
♫ Hunger is the only feeling

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:48.965
♫ Oświęcim

00:05:48.965 --> 00:05:52.034
♫ Like numbers on my arm it lingers

00:05:52.034 --> 00:05:54.322
♫ Oświęcim

00:05:54.322 --> 00:05:57.059
♫ I curse you with my broken fingers

00:05:57.059 --> 00:05:58.522
♫ Oświęcim

00:05:58.522 --> 00:05:59.657
♫ Oświęcim

00:05:59.657 --> 00:06:03.074
♫ Heart be hard as stone

00:06:04.716 --> 00:06:08.124
♫ Hatred is the greenest

00:06:08.124 --> 00:06:11.355
♫ Weed you've grown

00:06:11.355 --> 00:06:13.605
(applause)

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:17.672
Have a little respect for the next person,

00:06:17.672 --> 00:06:19.565
don't care who they are,

00:06:19.565 --> 00:06:23.732
because, actually, prejudice,
hate, winds up being death.

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:30.073
Give up yourself, you'll get it back.

00:06:32.269 --> 00:06:35.102
(sings in Polish)

